  "org.companies.body": "This lists the workspaces you belong to. An organization can own workspaces you are not a member of, and those are not shown here, because a workspace you cannot enter is not something we will confirm the existence of.",
  "org.companies.current": "You are here",
  "org.companies.open": "Open",
  "org.allowance.heading": "Plan and allowance",
  "org.allowance.body": "A plan is bought by the organization, not per seat, and the organization draws one monthly allowance that every workspace it owns shares. Spending in one workspace spends the same allowance the others draw on, and the figure is on the usage screen of whichever workspace you are in.",
  "org.access.heading": "Who has access",
  "org.access.body": "Access is granted per workspace today. A person invited to one workspace is not a member of the others, and their role is set in that workspace under Team. Editing access once for the whole organization is not built.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The navigation rail.
  //
  // THESE WERE HARDCODED ENGLISH INSIDE `nav-links.tsx` UNTIL NOW, which was a
  // standing §5b break the file's own header admitted to and could not fix:
  // `nav-links.tsx` is a client component and `t()` is server-only, so the
  // labels had nowhere to come from. They now resolve in `shell.tsx` and arrive
  // as props, which is the fix that header described and deferred.
  //
  // A LABEL HERE IS PAIRED WITH THE `h1` OF THE PAGE IT LEADS TO, and
  // `e2e/breadth.spec.ts` walks the pairs with `exact: true`. Most of those
  // headings are still hardcoded English in their page files, so a translated
  // rail above an English heading is a real and KNOWN inconsistency rather than
  // one introduced here: it already existed for 'Site builder', which was the
  // only translated heading in the rail. Widening the rail to six languages
  // makes the gap visible on twelve rows instead of one. The honest fix is the
  // headings, not these keys, and it is page-by-page work in files this rail
  // does not own.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The command center, which had none of these until 2026-08-27
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // Every string on the first screen of the product was authored in JSX, which
  // is §5b in full: the rail beside it renders in six languages while the page
  // it frames rendered in one, and a German reader saw Uebersicht over an
  // English heading.
  //
  // FOUR QUESTIONS, and the sections are named after them rather than after the
  // tables behind them: what is running, what needs me, what happened, what is
  // it costing. A person opening this wants to know whether to intervene, and a
  // heading naming a database concept makes them work that out for themselves.
  //
  // ONE PLURAL KEY ONLY, and the rest put the number in a LABEL rather than in a
  // sentence. That is better for a dashboard and it is also honest arithmetic:
  // each plural key costs sixteen strings across the six locales, four of which
  // carry a category English lacks, so a sentence with a count in it should earn
  // its place rather than be the default shape.
  "home.new": "Nothing has happened yet. This is a new company.",
  "home.recorded": {
    one: "{count} recorded event since this company was created.",
    other: "{count} recorded events since this company was created."
  },
  /*
      WHAT A NOTIFICATION SAYS, IN THE READER'S LANGUAGE.
  
      These were eight English sentences in a `Record` in `apps/app`, rendered on
      /activity and used as the title of every push notification. Both are surfaces
      a customer reads, so §5b applies and did not hold.
  
      ONE SENTENCE PER KIND, and the `Record` over the kind union stays: a kind
      added without a sentence is still a compile error. What changed is that the
      sentence is a key rather than the words. The ternary this arrangement
      replaced gave every kind but one the else branch, and told a company that had
      been STOPPED that it had a decision waiting.
    */
  "notification.headline.approval.waiting": "A contract is waiting on a decision",
  "notification.headline.message.received": "Someone emailed your company address",
  // NOT "waiting". The policy allowed it and nobody was asked, which is the one
  // thing this row exists to say.
  "notification.headline.run.auto_allowed": "Work ran without anybody being asked",
  "notification.headline.company.halted": "This company was stopped",
  // IT NAMES THE WAIT, because that is the only new information. The first line
  // already said a decision is waiting; this one exists because a day passed.
  "notification.headline.approval.escalated": "A decision has been waiting since yesterday",
  // NOT "waiting". A briefing asks nothing of anybody; it reports.
  "notification.headline.briefing.ready": "Your morning briefing is ready",
  // Says who did it, because "you were mentioned" reads as the product talking
  // and this is a person asking for a person.
  "notification.headline.comment.mentioned": "Somebody asked for you by name",
  // BOTH FACTS, because either alone is misleading: a goal that keeps failing is
  // not news if it is still running, and a goal that stopped is not news if
  // nobody knows why.
  "notification.headline.goal.thrashing": "A goal kept failing and was stopped",
  /*
    THE REST OF A PUSH NOTIFICATION. The title above is the headline; these are
    the two lines under it and the one shown when nothing is waiting. A push with
    a German title and an English body is worse than either on its own.
  */
  "notification.push.none.title": "Nothing is waiting in {company}",
  "notification.push.none.body": "You are up to date.",
  "notification.push.only": "In {company}.",
  "notification.push.more": {
    one: "In {company}, with {count} more waiting.",
    other: "In {company}, with {count} more waiting."
  },
